#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I develops the core mathematical structures and analytical tools used throughout theoretical and applied physics. Building on calculus and introductory linear algebra, the course introduces vector geometry, multivariable analysis, and differential equations in a unified framework designed for modeling physical systems.\nStudents learn how mathematical concepts such as vector spaces, linear transformations, multivariable functions, and differential equations provide the language and computational techniques used to describe motion, fields, and dynamical processes. The course emphasizes connections between abstract mathematical ideas and the physical principles they support, preparing students for advanced study in classical mechanics, electromagnetism, and related areas of physics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics that support later mathematical modeling in physics. Students study vector geometry in three-dimensional space, including equations of lines and planes and geometric relationships between them. These tools appear naturally in the description of trajectories, rays, and intersections of physical surfaces. Introductory set theory provides a precise language for describing mathematical structures and regions in space, supporting later work in multivariable analysis and probability.\nStudents then develop computational tools from linear algebra. Methods such as Gaussian elimination, matrix inverses, determinants, and LU factorization are used to analyze and solve systems of linear equations. These techniques arise when solving coupled equations in physical models, such as electrical circuits, mechanical systems with multiple interacting components, and discretized approximations of physical fields.\nThe course next develops the structure of vector spaces and linear transformations. Students study spans, bases, dimension, rank, and null spaces, and extend these ideas to abstract vector spaces. Linear maps and their matrix representations are examined, establishing the connection between geometric transformations and matrix algebra. These ideas culminate in the study of eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and matrix diagonalization, which are central tools for solving differential equations describing oscillations, normal modes of vibration, and stability of dynamical systems.\nThe course then turns to multivariable calculus and vector analysis. Students analyze functions of several variables, quadric surfaces, and coordinate transformations. Partial derivatives, gradients, and the multivariable chain rule are developed along with linear approximations and higher-order derivatives. These concepts describe how physical quantities such as temperature, pressure, or electric potential vary in space. Vector-valued functions and vector fields are studied together with curvature, divergence, and curl, providing the mathematical language used to describe velocity fields in fluid flow and electric and magnetic fields in electromagnetism.\nStudents then study multiple integration, focusing on double integrals and coordinate transformations such as polar coordinates. These techniques allow the computation of physical quantities distributed over regions, such as mass, charge, or probability density.\nThe final portion of the course introduces differential equations as models of dynamical systems. Students study first-order equations and qualitative behavior of solutions through phase-line analysis. Applications include motion under drag, gravitational attraction, electrical circuits, and population dynamics. The course concludes with second-order linear differential equations and oscillatory systems, including harmonic motion, damped oscillators, and resonance—fundamental mathematical models for vibrating strings, mechanical systems, and electromagnetic circuits." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1012 154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1013 "PS1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "866ae4e9-bcd7-58f6-a7f9-92b7b1bff22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1031 "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Polynomial Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1033 881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1034 0.6475284638017499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048130 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "81ceba80-b7bc-5614-b160-458b9437b8ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1031 "Modeling Capacitor Charge in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1033 1845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048396 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "9bd96abb-b01a-56d0-979c-1ad6fd88bbd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186231407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186231408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186231409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1031 "Modeling Current in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1033 6726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050226 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "37af8af0-066f-5b4b-80e9-c7639425b063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1031 "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Sinusoidal Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1033 883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1034 0.6283275861854207 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050378 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "44594723-00b7-53c1-8509-39704843a698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186230381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230376 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "00eff77d-799b-55ba-877d-1720b58cb296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230377 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{33}{50}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230377 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1bb9f4b-6c2e-5d6b-87d0-78e4c9a5b1f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230378 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c2d3d421-418f-598d-b619-f761de70afeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230379 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "353a8f78-ce46-5d98-86a4-c93a4fd5aa63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230380 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{10}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c9ff9c8-f28b-5bbc-b56a-9dca8a667374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230381 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{10}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231297 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231297 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fa/fae88c8da0b50da2bc025cbac0dbc376be3b67b69b59823746873c6e7d102dcc.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20c07bb4-e264-5674-a7f0-9a833f68d037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231297 #edb/ref 1074 "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(30I+500q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =30\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231298 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231298 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/08/085fe02b43301629af58379289fc9d1c49c90e71a83e0dd33f491ecf4b21bfc8.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ and the initial current is $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f2eeea4-7339-5f96-b3e0-fa160a791034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231298 #edb/ref 1074 "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=10I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac52\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I=e^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I=e^{-2t}$ and $I=e^{-t/2}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac94-\\dfrac52I-q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}I(0)-q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{40},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\nI'=-2Ae^{-2t}-\\dfrac12Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in $I'(0)=-\\dfrac1{40}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40}=-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\dfrac{7}{30}$ and $B=\\dfrac{59}{60}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\dfrac{7}{30}e^{-2t}+\\dfrac{59}{60}e^{-t/2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231299 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231299 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/323324bc8d0ab017d30bc2a37e5e5e66784d64da6309fc131d56cb576ed2b003.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc9247b8-2a3d-5559-af6a-0cf1d6fc7a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231299 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 8I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(8I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186231297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231407 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "459aac20-b2e4-5d77-b131-717c9745d43c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186231298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231408 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c6faa97d-4d8e-5d67-bb99-8aae6de5a840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186231299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231409 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186231820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling Current in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186231792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ae3b271d-f536-5674-bc90-24bf860c235c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c2c7cc0-6b5e-525e-9e79-08e37b20c7d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231577 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/16/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n12=40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(12) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =40\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q=12.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac407737-c497-5620-a022-43410b01e805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231583 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=12-5I-6q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =12-5I(0)-6q(0) \\\\  & =12-5\\cdot \\frac{9}{10}-6\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{10} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{24}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24}{5} & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{24}{5} & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{24}{5}=-3A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{33}{5}$ and $B=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0d/0d8f6711f76fa9e55f11087c99d0e626ebc665e0f44beeefe3f67e9d35159618.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4b2630f-dea5-5b20-90c5-3365567316a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231585 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1061 602721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09eed649-fd35-5633-ab45-26f9ff4df9a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1061 602722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d00c846-017e-5944-a878-8692d8e20406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1061 602725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b69ecfa-55d3-5851-a962-974fd7edd06b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1061 602729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dfab021e-6bf7-5ab4-8bd9-2af9f811ab5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1061 603155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10bd959b-3db6-58ef-8e40-eabd05f87aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1061 608077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6ed1a27-61c7-514f-ab23-a91a7edba38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to derive a second-order differential equation that governs the current $I(t)$ in an RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn a series RCL circuit, Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE(t)=V_R+V_C+V_L\n$$\n\nFor the three components:\n\n- Resistor: the voltage drop is proportional to the current,\n\n$$\nV_R=RI.\n$$\n\n- Capacitor: the charge $q$ and capacitor voltage satisfy $q=CV_C,$ so\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\n- Inductor: the voltage drop is proportional to the rate of change of current,\n\n$$\nV_L=L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into Kirchhoff's voltage law gives\n\n$$\nE=RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo obtain a differential equation in terms of $I$ only, we first differentiate both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(E) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(RI+\\frac{q}{C}+L\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ \\frac{dE}{dt} & =R\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{1}{C}\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+L\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q(t),$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ we get the second-order differential equation governing the current:\n\n$$\nL\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{C}I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ gives an equivalent form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac{R}{L}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{LC}I=\\dfrac{1}{L} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nLet's see a more concrete derivation in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1065 14791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9875681-c31f-535a-b3e2-7fcec32a16e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1064 "When we solve a second-order differential equation for the current $I(t),$ we often write the solution in the form\n\n$$\nI(t)=I_c(t)+I_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $I_c(t)$ is the **transient current**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n\n- $I_p(t)$ is the **steady-state current**, which governs the current’s long-term behavior.\n\nTo determine a unique solution for $I(t),$ we must specify two initial conditions, typically $I(0)$ and $I'(0).$\n\nThe current is often given directly as an initial condition, for example, $I(0)=I_0.$ We are also often given the initial *capacitor charge*, $q(0)=q_0.$\n\nTo find the other condition $I'(0),$ we use Kirchhoff's voltage law equation:\n\n$$\nE(t) = RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{1}{L}E - \\dfrac{R}{L}I - \\dfrac{q}{LC}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate at $t=0{:}$\n\n$$\nI'(0) = \\dfrac{1}{L}E(0) - \\dfrac{R}{L}I(0) - \\dfrac{1}{LC}q(0)\n$$\n\nSo, if the initial charge $q(0)$ and initial current $I(0)$ are given, we can compute $I'(0)$ by substituting those values into the formula above.\n\nIn the next slide, we'll determine the initial conditions of a differential equation that governs the current in a given circuit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1065 14808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6f06e46-ed1e-5e49-a9f8-679033a08a0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1067 "Initial Conditions for Solving the ODE Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose a circuit with a constant EMF of $E=8\\,\\textrm{V},$ resistance of $R=6\\,\\Omega,$ capacitance of $C=0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and inductance of $L=2\\,\\textrm{H},$ satisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law equation\n\n$$\n8=6I+4q+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's derive the differential equation for $I$ by differentiating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n0=6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ the differential equation for $I$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0.\n$$\n\nNow suppose the initial charge and current are $q(0)=\\dfrac12$ and $I(0)=1,$ respectively. Solving our first relation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 4-3I-2q.\n$$\n\nThen, the initial conditions for the current we use to solve the ODE are $I(0)=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =4-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\  & =4-3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the resulting initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0, \\qquad I(0) = 1, \\quad I'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nI(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1065 14901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02b0c1dd-4819-5337-bc05-870ded85d834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372823 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9596a372-debc-57f5-9e3c-81666bd27a19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372824 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372824 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eddd9073-1ad4-5279-b55d-a473698