#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372836 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372836 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d19eb6a-404e-54bc-b559-30dea0cde985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372837 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372837 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372837 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1b99eeb-efb4-5ea9-a273-a8358cc30301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372838 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "545cf06e-62be-5106-80f2-df116f2b8f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372839 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=-\\frac{24}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "699fb09b-da0b-5086-b8b5-517f256dd776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372840 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{24}{5}{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6b44c36-697c-54d6-b717-f23c70fba3be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/48/4852aaf3945331346a68fe3f967658665f71d81be0e28fd0d34fd965c7045596.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d96ca73-b13f-58db-8ae6-317919c23853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372911 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372912 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3b5f9ae5-8d9d-5c4b-9f0c-42d42f19c0c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372913 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf500311-ba0c-5251-a16d-ae6ce700c2e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372914 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "198292fb-0c70-5ea9-acb2-c7ed4d73d67f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372915 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372915 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372915 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17ba4a0e-ed7f-54bf-adfb-a5cffd850e8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372916 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372916 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372916 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b203598-dfae-5eba-a462-ef03871d8a31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372917 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de2d56c9-274b-5ce4-a18e-6325e1ac2464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4b612821f53f661975a8db889792b44a08aff2e5ba6d6cbc3ccb2992ee048b0b.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00e5ae50-dbfe-5fa5-ba4a-5369d82d33ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372960 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+6(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-12\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +12\\beta =100\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372961 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8a9f2ba6-6d39-5c9f-8012-929dccaa68cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372962 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{10}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372962 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372962 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b987082-a17c-53bc-bbed-1787c3c1d9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372963 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372963 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372963 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43efd276-fd28-518a-9979-3e368fa83dd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372964 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372964 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372964 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60480ab5-1701-50a6-a4b1-145226b4cb76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372965 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372965 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372965 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19932d86-ad56-5844-9fee-75926100c043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372966 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15c98267-2c10-515e-b941-3e73d588b194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28bb1b428a1c187234f0baae580c0fd83eba234c869225f78132f8d110d7a2bc.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7973b7d7-65db-5e2d-96ac-3fbab2d1e49a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372967 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.5\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.5}=2q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+2(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-15\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-8\\alpha -15\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-15\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -8\\alpha -15\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{48}{289}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{90}{289}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{48}{289})}^{2}+{(\\frac{90}{289})}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{17}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372968 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "392d36d7-2200-5901-9575-08503600f500" #edb/ref 175